when lori cash of new york took a job  with the u.s postal service  22 years ago it was for the security of  a government job  and a chance to work closely with the  public  to be able to service the public and do  something that i know  is helping people every day has just  always been appealing to me  but lately she's been hearing more  complaints from customers  dealing with delayed mail we'll have  someone mail a package in one city  and it takes three or four or five days  to get to the next city over  only 38 percent of first-class mail was  delivered on time  in late december according to postal  service court filings  that means everything from medications  to bill payments arriving late  and it's a problem that persists people  have got their bills delayed  when they've sent them to uh to  companies or utilities  companies or utilities or then charging  late fees  small businesses that once depended on  the u.s postal service to ship  products are also suffering maryland  boutique owner farzan jaeger  says many of the gift orders she  received before christmas  weren't delivered in time even pass way  past the holidays we would get emails  where is my gift did it receive did it  get delivered to the recipient  she now uses private shipping carriers  though they're more expensive  the u.s postal service lost more than 9  billion dollars in business in 2020  though the delivery delays that drove  away some customers  will likely improve after covid the  agency's long-running money troubles  will be harder to tackle  because its costs routinely exceed its  revenues  the man tasked with finding a solution  is postmaster general louis de joy  a trump mega donor who implemented cuts  to the postal service  at the height of the pandemic and in the  lead-up to last november's  mail-in voting detroit's latest plans  for the agency reportedly include  reducing services for local mail and  raising postage rates by nearly 10  percent  we can't do so much more for our  communities than what we do now  so if he's not willing to look outside  of  what we currently do if he just wants to  cut what we have and turn us into a  business  then he does not belong in the postal  service because you can't run this  service  with that close-minded attitude everyone  agrees the u.s postal service cannot  continue on its current footing  but the visions for how to help the  agency defer even as most believe  the service is worth saving heidi joe  castro al jazeera washington
 

